ublist of nodes, but each element of q ++ q' is in nodes, and the total weight of q ++ q' is at most the total weight of nodes because... hmm.\n\nActually, consider nodes = [a, b, c], q = [a, c], q' = [b]. They are disjoint. q ++ q' = [a, c, b]. Is wsum w [a, c, b] ≤ wsum w [a, b, c]? Yes, because wsum just sums the weights, and the set of elements is the same. So `wsum w [a, c, b] = wsum w [a, b, c]`.\n\nBut what if nodes = [a, b, c], q = [a], q' = [b, c]? Then q ++ q' = [a, b, c] = nodes, so wsum is equal.\n\nWhat if nodes = [a, b, c, d], q = [a, c], q' = [b]? Then q ++ q' = [a, c, b], which has the same set of elements as nodes (missing d). So wsum w [a, c, b] = wsum w [a, b, c, d] - w d ≤ wsum w [a, b, c, d].\n\nIn general, if q and q' are disjoint sublists of nodes, then the set of elements in q ++ q' is a subset of nodes (since each element of q and q' is in nodes, and they're disjoint). Since nodes is Nodup, the sum of weights of q ++ q' is at most the sum of weights of nodes.\n\nBut this reasoning requires going through sets, which is complicated in Lean without Mathlib.\n\nLet me think about a simpler approach.\n\nAlternative approach: Use the condition `hdiff` directly.\n\nLet me define:\n- `W(a) = k' * w a`\n- `W'(a) = k * w' a`\n\nThen `wdiff w w' k k' a = |W(a) - W'(a)|` (as Nat, with truncated subtraction).\n\nThe condition `(nodes.map (wdiff w w' k k')).sum ≤ 1` means that for all but at most one node, `W(a) = W'(a)`, and if they differ, the difference is 1.\n\nNow, consider `k' * wsum w q` and `k * wsum w' q'`.\n\nWe have:\n`k' * wsum w q = sum_{a∈q} k' * w a = sum_{a∈q} W(a)`\n`k * wsum w' q' = sum_{a∈q'} k * w' a = sum_{a∈q'} W'(a)`\n\nNow, if q and q' are disjoint, then:\n\n`sum_{a∈q} W(a) + sum_{a∈q'} W'(a)`\n\nFor each a ∈ q ∪ q', we're adding either W(a) or W'(a), but not both (since disjoint).\n\nNow, for all a ≠ a0, W(a) = W'(a). So:\n\nIf a0 ∉ q and a0 ∉ q', then for all a ∈ q ∪ q', W(a) = W'(a), so:\n`sum_{a∈q} W(a) + sum_{a∈q'} W'(a) = sum_{a∈q} W'(a) + sum_{a∈q'} W'(a) = sum_{a∈q∪q'} W'(a)`\n\nSince q and q' are disjoint sublists of nodes, the set of elements in q ∪ q' (as a set) is a subset of nodes. Since nodes is Nodup, this means `sum_{a∈q∪q'} W'(a) ≤ sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a) = k * wsum w' nodes`.\n\nBut we also have `2 * sum_{a∈q} W(a) > sum_{a∈nodes} W(a)` and `2 * sum_{a∈q'} W'(a) > sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a)`.\n\nSo `2 * (sum_{a∈q} W(a) + sum_{a∈q'} W'(a)) > sum_{a∈nodes} W(a) + sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a)`.\n\nBut `sum_{a∈q} W(a) + sum_{a∈q'} W'(a) ≤ sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a)` (from the argument above).\n\nSo `2 * sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a) > sum_{a∈nodes} W(a) + sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a)`, i.e., `sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a) > sum_{a∈nodes} W(a)`.\n\nBut `|sum_{a∈nodes} W(a) - sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a)| ≤ 1`, so this is only possible if `sum W' = sum W + 1`.\n\nSimilarly, we could get `sum W > sum W'` from the symmetric argument. Let me check...\n\nActually, wait. The inequality `sum_{a∈q} W(a) + sum_{a∈q'} W'(a) ≤ sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a)` only holds if a0 is not in q or q'. If a0 is in one of them, the analysis changes.\n\nLet me handle the cases more carefully.\n\nCase 1: a0 ∉ q and a0 ∉ q'. Then as argued above, we get `sum W' > sum W`, so `sum W' = sum W + 1`.\n\nCase 2: a0 ∈ q (and a0 ∉ q' since they're disjoint). Then:\n\n`sum_{a∈q} W(a) = W(a0) + sum_{a∈q\\{a0}} W(a)`\n`sum_{a∈q'} W'(a) = sum_{a∈q'} W'(a)` (all in S)\n\nNow, `W(a0) = W'(a0) ± 1` (since the difference is at most 1, and they're not equal).\n\nAnd for a ∈ S, `W(a) = W'(a)`.\n\nSo:\n`sum_{a∈q} W(a) + sum_{a∈q'} W'(a) = W(a0) + sum_{a∈q\\{a0}} W'(a) + sum_{a∈q'} W'(a)`\n`= W(a0) + sum_{a∈q\\{a0} ∪ q'} W'(a)`\n\nNow, `q\\{a0} ∪ q'` is a set of elements that is a subset of nodes \\ {a0} (since a0 is removed from q and not in q'). So:\n`sum_{a∈q\\{a0} ∪ q'} W'(a) ≤ sum_{a∈nodes \\ {a0}} W'(a)`\n\nThus:\n`sum_{a∈q} W(a) + sum_{a∈q'} W'(a) ≤ W(a0) + sum_{a∈nodes \\ {a0}} W'(a)`\n\nNow, `W(a0) = W'(a0) + d` where `d = 1` or `d = -1` (but as Nat, it's either `W'(a0) + 1` or `W(a0) + 1