al_g - 1`, i.e., `total_f_q + total_g_q' > total_g - 0.5`.\n\nSince everything is integer, `total_f_q + total_g_q' ≥ total_g`.\n\nBut we also have `total_f_q + total_g_q' ≤ total_g`. So `total_f_q + total_g_q' = total_g`.\n\nNow, `total_f_q + total_g_q' = total_g` means `sum_{a∈S} g(a) = sum_{a∈nodes} g(a)`. Since g(a) ≥ 0 for all a, this implies that the sum over the subset S equals the sum over the full set nodes. This is only possible if all elements not in S have weight 0. But elements not in S are exactly nodes \\ S. And since nodes is Nodup and S ⊆ nodes, nodes \\ S are the elements in nodes but not in S.\n\nBut wait, `sum_{a∈S} g(a) = sum_{a∈nodes} g(a)` implies `sum_{a∈nodes\\S} g(a) = 0`, so for all a ∈ nodes \\ S, g(a) = 0, i.e., k * w' a = 0. Since k > 0, this means w' a = 0 for all a ∉ S.\n\nBut then `total_g = sum_{a∈nodes} g(a) = sum_{a∈S} g(a)`. And `total_f = sum_{a∈nodes} f(a) = sum_{a∈S} f(a) + f(a0)` (if a0 ∈ nodes \\ S) or `sum_{a∈S} f(a)` (if a0 ∈ S, but we're in case 1 where a0 ∉ S).\n\nWait, in case 1, a0 ∉ S. So a0 ∈ nodes \\ S. Then f(a0) and g(a0) differ by at most 1, and g(a0) = 0 (since a0 ∉ S and all g(a) = 0 for a ∉ S). So g(a0) = 0, and f(a0) ≤ 1.\n\nBut then `total_f = sum_{a∈S} f(a) + f(a0) = total_g + f(a0) ≤ total_g + 1`.\n\nAnd `total_f_q + total_g_q' = total_g`. But `2 * total_f_q > total_f = total_g + f(a0)` and `2 * total_g_q' > total_g`.\n\nSo `2 * total_g > total_g + f(a0) + total_g = 2 * total_g + f(a0)`, i.e., `2 * total_g > 2 * total_g + f(a0)`, which means `0 > f(a0)`, impossible since f(a0) ≥ 0.\n\nSo case 1 leads to a contradiction.\n\nCase 2: a0 ∈ q (so a0 ∉ q'). Then:\n\n`total_f_q + total_g_q' = f(a0) + sum_{a∈q\\{a0}} f(a) + sum_{a∈q'} g(a)`\n\nFor a ∈ q\\{a0} ∪ q', we have a ≠ a0, so f(a) = g(a). Thus:\n`sum_{a∈q\\{a0}} f(a) + sum_{a∈q'} g(a) = sum_{a∈q\\{a0} ∪ q'} g(a)`\n\nLet `S' = q\\{a0} ∪ q'`. Then `total_f_q + total_g_q' = f(a0) + sum_{a∈S'} g(a)`.\n\nNow, S' ⊆ nodes \\ {a0}, so `sum_{a∈S'} g(a) ≤ sum_{a∈nodes\\{a0}} g(a) = total_g - g(a0)`.\n\nThus `total_f_q + total_g_q' ≤ f(a0) + total_g - g(a0) = total_g + (f(a0) - g(a0))`.\n\nSince f(a0) and g(a0) differ by at most 1, and f(a0) ≠ g(a0) (since a0 is the differing node), we have |f(a0) - g(a0)| = 1.\n\nSo `total_f_q + total_g_q' ≤ total_g ± 1`.\n\nNow, from the majority conditions:\n`2 * total_f_q > total_f` and `2 * total_g_q' > total_g`.\n\nAdding: `2 * (total_f_q + total_g_q') > total_f + total_g`.\n\nNow, `total_f = sum_{a∈nodes} f(a) = sum_{a∈S'} f(a) + f(a0) + sum_{a∈nodes\\(S'∪{a0})} f(a)`.\n\nSince for a ∈ S', f(a) = g(a), we have `sum_{a∈S'} f(a) = sum_{a∈S'} g(a)`.\n\nAnd for a ∉ S' ∪ {a0}, we have a ≠ a0, so f(a) = g(a). Thus:\n`total_f = sum_{a∈S'} g(a) + f(a0) + sum_{a∉S'∪{a0}} g(a) = total_g - g(a0) + f(a0) = total_g + (f(a0) - g(a0))`.\n\nSo `total_f + total_g = 2 * total_g + (f(a0) - g(a0))`.\n\nThus: `2 * (total_f_q + total_g_q') > 2 * total_g + (f(a0) - g(a0))`.\n\nSo `total_f_q + total_g_q' > total_g + (f(a0) - g(a0))/2`.\n\nSince total_f_q + total_g_q' is integer, and `f(a0) - g(a0)` is either 1 or -1 (but as Nat, it's either f(a0) = g(a0) + 1 or g(a0) = f(a0) + 1), we have two subcases.\n\nSubcase 2a: f(a0) = g(a0) + 1. Then `f(a0) - g(a0) = 1` (as integer, but in Nat this is tricky).\n\nActually, let me work in integers. The issue is that wdiff uses Nat subtraction, which is truncated.\n\nLet me think about this differently. Let me use the property that `wdiff w w' k k' a` is the absolute difference.\n\nIf `(nodes.map (wdiff w w' k k')).sum ≤ 1`, then for each a, `wdiff w w' k k' a ≤ 1`, and at most one a has `wdiff w w' k k' a = 1`.\n\nNow, `wdiff w w' k k' a = 0` iff `k * w a ≤ k' * w' a` and `k' * w' a - k * w a = 0`, or `k' * w' a ≤ k * w a` and `k * w a - k' * w' a = 0`. In either case, `k * w a = k' * w' a`.\n\nSo `wdiff w w' k k' a = 0` ↔ `k * w a = k' * w' a`.\n\nAnd `wdiff w w' k k' a = 1` means the two values differ by exactly 1 (and the larger one minus the smaller one = 1).\n\nNow, let me use this to simplify the proof.\n\nLet me define:\n- `eq_nodes` = list of nodes where `k * w a = k' * w' a`\n- `diff_node` = the node (if any) where they differ by 1\n\nSince `(nodes.map (wdiff w w' k k')).sum ≤ 1`, we know that all nodes except possibly one satisfy `k * w a = k' * w' a`, and the differing node (if any) has difference exactly 1.\n\nNow, let me try to write the proof using this characterization.\n\nActually, I think I can avoid this complexity by using the following simpler argument:\n\nConsider `k' * wsum w q + k * wsum w' q'`.\n\nWe have:\n- `k' * wsum w q > (k' * wsum w nodes) / 2` (from the majority condition, since k' > 0)\n- `k * wsum w' q' > (k * wsum w' nodes) / 2` (from the majority condition, since k > 0)\n\nAdding: `k' * wsum w q + k * wsum w' q' > (k' * wsum w nodes + k * wsum w' nodes) / 2`\n\nNow, `k' * wsum w nodes = sum_{a∈nodes} k' * w a` and `k * wsum w' nodes = sum_{a∈nodes} k * w' a`.\n\nThe difference between these sums is at most 1 (by the hdiff condition).\n\nSo `k' * wsum w nodes + k * wsum w' nodes ≤ 2 * max(k' * wsum w nodes, k * wsum w' nodes) + 1`.\n\nThis doesn't directly help.\n\nLet me try a completely different approach. Let me look at the original proof from the UPaxos paper.\n\nActually, I think the key insight I'm missing is simpler. Let me re-read the hint:\n\n\"scale both majorities by k and k' respectively; if q and q' were disjoint then wsum over q ++ q' would be a sublist sum bounded by the total; the single node where the scaled weights differ by 1 gives the contradiction.\"\n\nMaybe \"wsum over q ++ q'\" is bounded because q ++ q' is a sublist of nodes ++ nodes (which is always true), and wsum over nodes ++ nodes is 2 * wsum over nodes. But that gives a bound of 2 * total, not total.\n\nWait, maybe the hint means something different. Let me re-read: \"if q and q' were disjoint then wsum over q ++ q' would be a sublist sum bounded by the total\"\n\nMaybe it means: if q and q' are disjoint, then q ++ q' cannot be a sublist of nodes (since nodes is Nodup and q, q' are disjoint sublists, their concatenation would have more elements than nodes, or would have duplicates). So wsum over q ++ q' is not bounded by the total in the sublist sense. But...\n\nActually, I think the argument is:\n\nIf q and q' are disjoint sublists of nodes, then `q ++ q'` as a multiset is a sub-multiset of nodes (since each element appears at most once in nodes, and q and q' are disjoint). Therefore, the sum of weights of `q ++ q'` is at most the sum of weights of nodes. This is because for non-negative weights, the sum over a sub-multiset is at most the sum over the full multiset.\n\nBut `q ++ q'` is not necessarily a sublist of nodes, so we can't use `Sublist.sum_le_sum`. However, we can use the fact that the set of elements in `q ++ q'` is a subset of nodes, and since weights are non-negative, the sum over a subset is at most the sum over the whole set.\n\nBut this requires going through sets/membership, which is messy in core Lean.\n\nLet me try yet another approach. Instead of using the subset sum bound, let me use the sublist structure directly.\n\nSince q and q' are sublists of nodes, we can use induction on the sublist derivation to prove things about them.\n\nActually, let me try to prove the theorem using the following lemma:\n\nIf `l₁ <+ nodes` and `l₂ <+ nodes` and `l₁` and `l₂` are disjoint (no common elements), then `wsum w (l₁ ++ l₂) ≤ wsum w nodes`.\n\nIs this true? Let me think...\n\nConsider nodes = [a, b, c], l₁ = [a, c], l₂ = [b]. Then l₁ ++ l₂ = [a, c, b]. Is wsum w [a, c, b] ≤ wsum w [a, b, c]? Yes, because both are the same set of elements, so the sums are equal.\n\nConsider nodes = [a, b, c], l₁ = [a], l₂ = [b, c]. Then l₁ ++ l₂ = [a, b, c] = nodes. So wsum = wsum.\n\nConsider nodes = [a, b, c], l₁ = [a, b], l₂ = [c]. Then l₁ ++ l₂ = [a, b, c] = nodes.\n\nIn general, if l₁ and l₂ are disjoint sublists of nodes (where nodes is Nodup), then the multiset of l₁ ++ l₂ is a sub-multiset of nodes (each element appears at most as many times as in nodes, which is at most 1). So the sum of weights of l₁ ++ l₂ is at most the sum of weights of nodes.\n\nBut to prove this in Lean, I need to use the fact that l₁ and l₂ are sublists of nodes and disjoint, which implies that each element of l₁ ++ l₂ is in nodes and appears at most once.\n\nActually, I think there's a simpler way. Since l₁ <+ nodes and l₂ <+ nodes, and l₁ and l₂ are disjoint, we can show that l₁ ++ l₂ <+ nodes ++ nodes (by Sublist.append). But that gives wsum w (l₁ ++ l₂) ≤ wsum w (nodes ++ nodes) = 2 * wsum w nodes, which is too weak.\n\nHmm.\n\nLet me try to prove the subset sum property directly by induction on nodes.\n\nLemma: If l <+ nodes and nodes.Nodup, then for any a, if a ∉ l, then l <+ nodes.erase a? No, that's not right.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. I'll prove:\n\nLemma `sublist_sum_le` (l <+ nodes) → wsum w l ≤ wsum w nodes.\n\nThis is true by induction on the Sublist derivation, as I noted earlier. Let me just prove this.\n\nThen, for the disjoint case, I need to show that `wsum w (q ++ q') ≤ wsum w nodes`. But q ++ q' is not necessarily a sublist of nodes.\n\nWait, but maybe I don't need that. Let me reconsider the proof.\n\nGoing back to the contradiction argument:\n\nAssume q and q' are disjoint. Then:\n\nConsider `k' * wsum w q + k * wsum w' q'`.\n\nFrom the majority conditions:\n`2 * k' * wsum w q > k' * wsum w nodes` (1)\n`2 * k * wsum w' q' > k * wsum w' nodes` (2)\n\nAdding (1) and (2):\n`2 * (k' * wsum w q + k * wsum w' q') > k' * wsum w nodes + k * wsum w' nodes` (3)\n\nNow, from the hdiff condition:\n`(nodes.map (wdiff w w' k k')).sum ≤ 1`\n\nThis means `sum_{a∈nodes} |k' * w a - k * w' a| ≤ 1` (as Nat).\n\nNow, I claim that `k' * wsum w nodes + k * wsum w' nodes ≥ 2 * (k' * wsum w q + k * wsum w' q') - 1`.\n\nWhy? Because...\n\nActually, let me try to prove this directly.\n\nFor each a ∈ nodes:\n- If a ∈ q, then `k' * w a` contributes to the LHS sum.\n- If a ∈ q', then `k * w' a` contributes to the LHS sum.\n- If a ∉ q ∪ q', then neither contributes.\n\nBut the RHS involves `k' * wsum w q + k * wsum w' q' = sum_{a∈q} k' * w a + sum_{a∈q'} k * w' a`.\n\nNow, for a ∈ q ∩ q' (which is empty by assumption), both would contribute. For a ∈ q \\ q', only the first contributes. For a ∈ q' \\ q, only the second contributes.\n\nSo `k' * wsum w nodes + k * wsum w' nodes = sum_{a∈q} k' * w a + sum_{a∈q'} k * w' a + sum_{a∉q∪q'} (k' * w a + k * w' a)`.\n\nThus:\n`k' * wsum w nodes + k * wsum w' nodes = (k' * wsum w q + k * wsum w' q') + sum_{a∉q∪q'} (k' * w a + k * w' a)`.\n\nNow, `sum_{a∉q∪q'} (k' * w a + k * w' a) ≥ 0`, so:\n`k' * wsum w nodes + k * wsum w' nodes ≥ k' * wsum w q + k * wsum w' q'`.\n\nBut this gives `k' * wsum w q + k * wsum w' q' ≤ k' * wsum w nodes + k * wsum w' nodes`, which combined with (3) gives:\n\n`2 * (k' * wsum w q + k * wsum w' q') > k' * wsum w q + k * wsum w' q' + sum_{a∉q∪q'} (k' * w a + k * w' a)`\n\nSo `k' * wsum w q + k * wsum w' q' > sum_{a∉q∪q'} (k' * w a + k * w' a)`.\n\nThis doesn't give a contradiction.\n\nHmm, I'm going in circles. Let me try to write the proof in a completely different way.\n\nLet me look at this from the perspective of the scaled weight functions.\n\nDefine `W(a) = k' * w a` and `W'(a) = k * w' a`.\n\nThe condition `(nodes.map (wdiff w w' k k')).sum ≤ 1` means:\n`sum_{a∈nodes} |W(a) - W'(a)| ≤ 1` (as Nat, with truncated subtraction).\n\nNow, q is a majority under w, meaning `2 * wsum w q > wsum w nodes`.\nMultiply by k': `2 * sum_{a∈q} W(a) > sum_{a∈nodes} W(a)`.\n\nSimilarly, q' is a majority under w', meaning `2 * wsum w' q' > wsum w' nodes`.\nMultiply by k: `2 * sum_{a∈q'} W'(a) > sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a)`.\n\nNow, suppose q and q' are disjoint. Then the sets of elements in q and q' are disjoint.\n\nLet `T = sum_{a∈q} W(a) + sum_{a∈q'} W'(a)`.\n\nWe have:\n`2 * sum_{a∈q} W(a) > sum_{a∈nodes} W(a)` (A)\n`2 * sum_{a∈q'} W'(a) > sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a)` (B)\n\nAdding: `2T > sum_{a∈nodes} W(a) + sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a)` (C)\n\nNow, `sum_{a∈nodes} W(a) + sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a) = sum_{a∈q} W(a) + sum_{a∈q'} W'(a) + sum_{a∉q∪q'} (W(a) + W'(a)) + (if a0 ∈ nodes\\q∪q' then W(a0)+W'(a0) else 0)`.\n\nWait, this is getting complicated because of the potential differing node.\n\nLet me use a different approach. Let me use the fact that `|W(a) - W'(a)| ≤ 1` for all a, and `sum |W(a) - W'(a)| ≤ 1`.\n\nThis implies that for all a ≠ a0, `W(a) = W'(a)`, and for a = a0 (if it exists), `|W(a0) - W'(a0)| = 1`.\n\nNow, consider T = `sum_{a∈q} W(a) + sum_{a∈q'} W'(a)`.\n\nCase 1: a0 ∉ q and a0 ∉ q'. Then for all a ∈ q ∪ q', W(a) = W'(a). So:\n`T = sum_{a∈q∪q'} W(a) = sum_{a∈q∪q'} W'(a)`.\n\nNow, `sum_{a∈nodes} W(a) + sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a) = 2 * sum_{a∈q∪q'} W(a) + sum_{a∉q∪q'} (W(a) + W'(a))`.\n\nSince for a ∉ q ∪ q', a ≠ a0 (because a0 is not in q ∪ q'), we have W(a) = W'(a). So:\n`sum_{a∉q∪q'} (W(a) + W'(a)) = 2 * sum_{a∉q∪q'} W(a)`.\n\nThus:\n`sum_{a∈nodes} W(a) + sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a) = 2 * sum_{a∈q∪q'} W(a) + 2 * sum_{a∉q∪q'} W(a) = 2 * sum_{a∈nodes} W(a)`.\n\nWait, that can't be right. Let me recalculate.\n\n`sum_{a∈nodes} W(a) = sum_{a∈q∪q'} W(a) + sum_{a∉q∪q'} W(a)` (since q∪q' ⊆ nodes).\n\n`sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a) = sum_{a∈q∪q'} W'(a) + sum_{a∉q∪q'} W'(a)`.\n\nSince for a ∈ q∪q', W(a) = W'(a), and for a ∉ q∪q', a ≠ a0, so W(a) = W'(a), we have:\n`sum_{a∈q∪q'} W(a) = sum_{a∈q∪q'} W'(a)` and `sum_{a∉q∪q'} W(a) = sum_{a∉q∪q'} W'(a)`.\n\nSo `sum_{a∈nodes} W(a) = sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a)`.\n\nThus `sum_{a∈nodes} W(a) + sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a) = 2 * sum_{a∈nodes} W(a)`.\n\nNow, from (C): `2T > 2 * sum_{a∈nodes} W(a)`, so `T > sum_{a∈nodes} W(a)`.\n\nBut `T = sum_{a∈q∪q'} W(a) ≤ sum_{a∈nodes} W(a)` (since q∪q' ⊆ nodes and all weights are non-negative).\n\nContradiction!\n\nSo case 1 is impossible. Therefore a0 ∈ q ∪ q'.\n\nCase 2: a0 ∈ q (so a0 ∉ q'). Then:\n\n`T = W(a0) + sum_{a∈q\\{a0}} W(a) + sum_{a∈q'} W'(a)`.\n\nFor a ∈ q\\{a0} ∪ q', we have a ≠ a0, so W(a) = W'(a). Thus:\n`sum_{a∈q\\{a0}} W(a) + sum_{a∈q'} W'(a) = sum_{a∈q\\{a0} ∪ q'} W'(a)`.\n\nLet `S = q\\{a0} ∪ q'`. Then `T = W(a0) + sum_{a∈S} W'(a)`.\n\nNow, `sum_{a∈nodes} W(a) = W(a0) + sum_{a∈S} W(a) + sum_{a∈nodes\\(S∪{a0})} W(a)`.\nSince for a ∈ S, W(a) = W'(a), and for a ∉ S∪{a0}, a ≠ a0, so W(a) = W'(a):\n`sum_{a∈nodes} W(a) = W(a0) + sum_{a∈S} W'(a) + sum_{a∈nodes\\(S∪{a0})} W'(a) = W(a0) + sum_{a∈nodes\\{a0}} W'(a)`.\n\nSimilarly:\n`sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a) = W'(a0) + sum_{a∈S} W'(a) + sum_{a∈nodes\\(S∪{a0})} W'(a) = W'(a0) + sum_{a∈nodes\\{a0}} W'(a)`.\n\nSo:\n`sum_{a∈nodes} W(a) + sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a) = W(a0) + W'(a0) + 2 * sum_{a∈nodes\\{a0}} W'(a)`.\n\nNow, `T = W(a0) + sum_{a∈S} W'(a)`.\n\nFrom (C): `2T > sum_{a∈nodes} W(a) + sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a) = W(a0) + W'(a0) + 2 * sum_{a∈nodes\\{a0}} W'(a)`.\n\nSo `2W(a0) + 2 * sum_{a∈S} W'(a) > W(a0) + W'(a0) + 2 * sum_{a∈nodes\\{a0}} W'(a)`.\n\nThus `W(a0) + 2 * sum_{a∈S} W'(a) > W'(a0) + 2 * sum_{a∈nodes\\{a0}} W'(a)`.\n\nNow, `sum_{a∈nodes\\{a0}} W'(a) = sum_{a∈S} W'(a) + sum_{a∈nodes\\(S∪{a0})} W'(a)`.\n\nSo:\n`W(a0) + 2 * sum_{a∈S} W'(a) > W'(a0) + 2 * sum_{a∈S} W'(a) + 2 * sum_{a∈nodes\\(S∪{a0})} W'(a)`.\n\nCancel `2 * sum_{a∈S} W'(a)`:\n`W(a0) > W'(a0) + 2 * sum_{a∈nodes\\(S∪{a0})} W'(a)`.\n\nSince `2 * sum_{a∈nodes\\(S∪{a0})} W'(a) ≥ 0`, we have `W(a0) > W'(a0)`.\n\nBut `|W(a0) - W'(a0)| = 1`, so `W(a0) = W'(a0) + 1`.\n\nThus `W'(a0) + 1 > W'(a0) + 2 * sum_{a∈nodes\\(S∪{a0})} W'(a)`, so `1 > 2 * sum_{a∈nodes\\(S∪{a0})} W'(a)`.\n\nSince the RHS is an even non-negative integer, `2 * sum_{a∈nodes\\(S∪{a0})} W'(a)` must be 0. So `sum_{a∈nodes\\(S∪{a0})} W'(a) = 0`.\n\nThis means for all a ∈ nodes \\ (S ∪ {a0}), W'(a) = 0. Since W'(a) = k * w' a and k > 0, this means w' a = 0 for all such a.\n\nNow, `sum_{a∈nodes\\{a0}} W'(a) = sum_{a∈S} W'(a) + 0 = sum_{a∈S} W'(a)`.\n\nSo `sum_{a∈nodes} W'(a) = W'(a0) + sum_{a∈S} W'(a)`.\n\nAnd `sum_{a∈nodes} W(a) = W(a0) + sum_{a∈S} W'(a) = (W'(a0) + 1) + sum_{a∈S} W'(a) = sum_{a