erive a contradiction with `sum |k'*w - k*w'| ≤ 1`.\n\nLet me use the following identity:\n\n`k' * wsum w nodes + k * wsum w' nodes - 2(k' * wsum w q + k * wsum w' q')`\n\nExpand:\n`= k'*(wsum w nodes - 2*wsum w q) + k*(wsum w' nodes - 2*wsum w' q')`\n\nSince `2*wsum w q > wsum w nodes`, in integers, `wsum w nodes - 2*wsum w q < 0`. But in Nat, this is 0.\n\nSo this identity doesn't work in Nat.\n\nLet me use integer arithmetic. I'll convert everything to `Int` and work there.\n\nActually, I can avoid subtraction by using the following:\n\nFrom `2*wsum w q > wsum w nodes`, we have `2*wsum w q ≥ wsum w nodes + 1`.\nFrom `2*wsum w' q' > wsum w' nodes`, we have `2*wsum w' q' ≥ wsum w' nodes + 1`.\n\nNow:\n`2(k' * wsum w q + k * wsum w' q') ≥ k'(wsum w nodes + 1) + k(wsum w' nodes + 1) = k'*wsum w nodes + k*wsum w' nodes + k' + k`.\n\nSo `2S ≥ k'*wsum w nodes + k*wsum w' nodes + k' + k`. (1)\n\nNow, I need an upper bound on S. Let me use the wdiff condition.\n\nFor each a, define `d(a) = wdiff w w' k k' a`.\n\nWe have `k' * w a ≤ k * w' a + d(a)` and `k * w' a ≤ k' * w a + d(a)`.\n\nNow:\n`k' * wsum w q = sum_{a∈q} k' * w a ≤ sum_{a∈q} (k * w' a + d(a)) = k * wsum w' q + sum_{a∈q} d(a)`.\n`k * wsum w' q' ≤ k' * wsum w q' + sum_{a∈q'} d(a)`.\n\nSo:\n`S ≤ k * wsum w' q + k' * wsum w q' + sum_{a∈q∪q'} d(a)`.\n\nNow, I need to bound `k * wsum w' q + k' * wsum w q'`.\n\nSince q and q' are disjoint sublists of nodes, and both are Nodup (since nodes is Nodup and they're sublists), we have:\n\n`wsum w' q + wsum w q' = sum_{a∈q} w' a + sum_{a∈q'} w a`.\n\nBut this doesn't directly relate to `wsum w' nodes + wsum w nodes`.\n\nHowever, note that for any a, `w' a + w a` is the sum of the two weights. And `k * wsum w' q + k' * wsum w q' = sum_{a∈q} k*w' a + sum_{a∈q'} k'*w a`.\n\nSince q and q' are disjoint, each element of nodes appears in at most one of q or q'. So:\n\n`sum_{a∈q} k*w' a + sum_{a∈q'} k'*w a ≤ sum_{a∈nodes} max(k*w' a, k'*w a)`? No.\n\nActually, I can bound this by: