=>\n              -- a is later in the list, but we need the first element to be a\n              -- Actually, if a ∈ a :: l₁', then a = a (head) or a ∈ l₁' (tail)\n              -- But a is the head of the cons, so a :: l₁' starts with a\n              -- The only way a ∈ a :: l₁' is if a = a (always true) or a ∈ l₁'\n              -- But if a = a, then the head matches\n              sorry\n          | cons_cons a' h₁' =>\n            simp at ha₁\n            cases ha₁ with\n            | head => exact ⟨l₁', rfl, h₁'⟩\n            | tail _ ha₁' => sorry\n      sorry\n    · -- a ∉ l₁\n      by_cases ha₂ : a ∈ l₂\n      · -- a ∈ l₂, symmetric to above\n        sorry\n      · -- a ∉ l₁ and a ∉ l₂, so both are sublists of nodes\n        have h₁' : l₁ <+ nodes := by\n          cases h₁ with\n          | slnil => exact .slnil _\n          | cons a' h₁' =>\n            -- l₁ <+ a :: nodes and a ∉ l₁\n            -- So l₁ <+ nodes\n            cases l₁ with\n            | nil => exact .slnil _\n            | cons b l₁' =>\n              cases h₁ with\n              | cons a' h₁' => exact h₁'\n              | cons_cons a' h₁' => exact h₁'\n              | slnil => simp at ha₁\n          | cons_cons a' h₁' =>\n            -- l₁ <+ a :: nodes and a ∉ l₁\n            -- Need to show l₁ <+ nodes\n            sorry\n        have h₂' : l₂ <+ nodes := by\n          sorry\n        exact IH hnd h₁' h₂' hdisj"}]}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 5.073125001217704, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Edited", "message": "Weights.lean", "warnings": [], "suffix": ""}}}
{"id": "50a3ed74-6040-419b-9bac-494c5fdf551b", "sessionId": "4cdae12a-58f0-3dfe-f9fa-2f6401f0bef8", "turnId": "f07beda4-c93f-4203-b893-c849a10cd745", "createdAt": 1788652003802, "updatedAt": 1788652056372, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "This approach with the induction on nodes is getting very messy. Let me think of a simpler proof for `sum_two_disjoint_sublists_le`.\n\nActually, I think I can use the existing lemma `Sublist.subset` which gives `l₁ ⊆ nodes` from `l₁ <+ nodes`. And then use the `Subset` relation to reason about membership.\n\nBut `Subset` is defined as `∀ {a}, a ∈ l₁ → a ∈ nodes`. So from `h₁.subset` and `h₂.subset`, I know that every element of l₁ and l₂ is in nodes.\n\nNow, I want to show `(l₁.map f).sum + (l₂.map f).sum ≤ (nodes.map f).sum`.\n\nSince l₁ and l₂ are disjoint, the multiset union has each element at most once. But nodes might have duplicates? No, nodes is Nodup. So each element appears at most once in nodes, and at most once in l₁ (since l₁ is a sublist of a Nodup list, l₁ is also Nodup). Similarly for l₂.\n\nSo the multiset of l₁ ++ l₂ has each element at most once, and each element is in nodes. Therefore, the sum over l₁ ++ l₂ is at most the sum over nodes.\n\nBut to prove this, I can use `wsum_append` and `wsum_sublist_le`:\n`(l₁.map f).sum + (l₂.map f).sum = (l₁.map f).sum + (l₂.map f).sum`.\n\nNow, `l₁.map f` is a sublist of `nodes.map f`? Not necessarily, because the order might differ. But `(l₁.map f).sum ≤ (nodes.map f).sum` by `wsum_sublist_le` (since `l₁ <+ nodes` implies `l₁.map f <+ nodes.map f`).\n\nWait, `Sublist.map` gives `l₁.map f <+ nodes.map f`. So `(l₁.map f).sum ≤ (nodes.map f).sum` by `wsum_sublist_le`. Similarly for l₂.\n\nSo `(l₁.map f).sum + (l₂.map f).sum ≤ (nodes.map f).sum + (nodes.map f).sum = 2*(nodes.map f).sum`.\n\nThis is too weak. I need the stronger bound `(l₁.map f).sum + (l₂.map f).sum ≤ (nodes.map f).sum`.\n\nBut as I noted earlier, this is not true in general! Consider nodes = [a, b], l₁ = [a], l₂ = [b]. Then sum = f a + f b = sum nodes. OK.\n\nConsider nodes = [a, b, c], l₁ = [a], l₂ = [b]. Then sum = f a + f b ≤ f a + f b + f c = sum nodes. OK.\n\nBut what about nodes = [a, b, c], l₁ = [a, b], l₂ = [b, c]? They're not disjoint (b is in both). But we assume disjoint.\n\nWhat about nodes = [a, b, c, d], l₁ = [a, c], l₂ = [b]? Sum = f a + f c + f b ≤ f a + f b + f c + f d. OK.\n\nI think the inequality IS true: if l₁ and l₂ are disjoint sublists of a Nodup list nodes, then the sum of l₁ plus the sum of l₂ is at most the sum of nodes. This is because the multiset of l₁ ++ l₂ is a sub-multiset of nodes (each element appears at most as many times as in nodes, which is 1).\n\nProof: Since l₁ <+ nodes and l₂ <+ nodes, and l₁ and l₂ are disjoint, each element of l₁ or l₂ appears in nodes. Since nodes is Nodup, each element appears at most once in nodes. Therefore, each element appears at most once in l₁ ++ l₂. So the multiset of l₁ ++ l₂ is a sub-multiset of nodes. Since f is non-negative, the sum over the sub-multiset is ≤ the sum over the full multiset.\n\nBut formalizing this in Lean is complex. Let me try a different approach.\n\nActually, I think I can avoid this lemma entirely by using a different proof strategy. Let me reconsider the main proof.\n\nGoing back to the key inequality: `S + X ≤ k'*W + k*W'`.\n\nI had `S + X = sum_{q∪q'} (k'*w a + k*w' a)`.\n\nNow, `sum_{q∪q'} (k'*w a + k*w' a) = k'*wsum w (q∪q') + k*wsum w' (q∪q')`.\n\nI can bound this by `k'*W + k*W'` if I can show:\n`wsum w (q∪q') ≤ W` and `wsum w' (q∪q') ≤ W'`.\n\nBut `wsum w (q∪q') = wsum w q + wsum w q'` (by `wsum_append`).\n\nNow, `wsum w q ≤ W` (by `wsum_sublist_le`). And `wsum w q' ≤ W`. But this gives `wsum w q + wsum w q' ≤ 2W`, not `≤ W`.\n\nHowever, I can use a different decomposition:\n\n`k'*wsum w q + k*wsum w' q' = S` and `k'*wsum w q' + k*wsum w' q = X`.\n\nSo `S + X = k'*wsum w q + k*wsum w' q' + k'*wsum w q' + k*wsum w' q`\n`= k'*(wsum w q + wsum w q') + k*(wsum w' q + wsum w' q')`.\n\nNow, `wsum w q + wsum w q'` is the sum of weights of q and q' under w. Since q and q' are disjoint, this is at most W. Similarly, `wsum w' q + wsum w' q' ≤ W'`.\n\nSo `S + X ≤ k'*W + k*W'`.\n\nThis is exactly the subset sum lemma I was trying to prove, but applied to the pair (q, q') with weight function w, and to (q, q') with weight function w'.\n\nWait, but I need `wsum w q + wsum w q' ≤ W`. This is `(q.map w).sum + (q'.map w).sum ≤ (nodes.map w).sum`. This is exactly the statement of `sum_two_disjoint_sublists_le` with `f = w`.\n\nSo I do need this lemma. But maybe I can prove it more simply.\n\nLet me try a direct proof by induction on the derivation of `h₁` and `h₂`.\n\nActually, I think there's a simpler proof using `Sublist.subset` and `Nodup`.\n\nSince `h₁.subset : l₁ ⊆ nodes` and `h₂.subset : l₂ ⊆ nodes`, every element of l₁ or l₂ is in nodes. Since `hnd : nodes.Nodup`, each element appears at most once in nodes. Since l₁ and l₂ are disjoint, each element appears at most once in l₁ ++ l₂.\n\nNow, to show `(l₁.map f).sum + (l₂.map f).sum ≤ (nodes.map f).sum`, I can use the following:\n\nLet `g(a) = count(a, l₁) * f a + count(a, l₂) * f a`. Then the LHS is `sum_a g(a)` and the RHS is `sum_a count(a, nodes) * f a`. Since `count(a, l₁) + count(a, l₂) ≤ count(a, nodes)` (because l₁ and l₂ are disjoint sublists of a Nodup list), we have `g(a) ≤ count(a, nodes) * f a` for each a. Summing gives the result.\n\nBut this requires `count` and `Finset.sum`, which are Mathlib.\n\nOK, let me try yet another approach. Maybe I can use the following:\n\nSince `l₁ <+ nodes`, there exists `m₁` such that `l₁ ++ m₁ = nodes`. Similarly, `l₂ ++ m₂ = nodes`.\n\nThen `(nodes.map f).sum = (l₁.map f).sum + (m₁.map f).sum = (l₂.map f).sum + (m₂.map f).sum`.\n\nNow, `(l₁.map f).sum + (l₂.map f).sum = ((l₁.map f).sum + (m₁.map f).sum) + ((l₂.map f).sum + (m₂.map f).sum) - ((m₁.map f).sum + (m₂.map f).sum)`\n`= 2*(nodes.map f).sum - ((m₁.map f).sum + (m₂.map f).sum)`.\n\nThis doesn't help.\n\nLet me try induction on `l₁` and `l₂` simultaneously.\n\nActually, I think the simplest proof is by induction on `nodes`:\n\nBase: nodes = []. Then l₁ = l₂ = [], trivial.\n\nInductive step: nodes = a :: nodes'.\nWe have l₁ <+ a :: nodes' and l₂ <+ a :: nodes'.\n\nCase 1: a ∈ l₁. Then l₁ = a :: l₁' where l₁' <+ nodes'. Since l₂ is disjoint from l₁, a ∉ l₂. So l₂ <+ nodes'. By IH, `(l₁'.map f).sum + (l₂.map f).sum ≤ (nodes'.map f).sum`. Then `(l₁.map f).sum + (l₂.map f).sum = f a + (l₁'.map f).sum + (l₂.map f).sum ≤ f a + (nodes'.map f).sum = ((a::nodes').map f).sum`.\n\nCase 2: a ∈ l₂. Symmetric.\n\nCase 3: a ∉ l₁ and a ∉ l₂. Then l₁ <+ nodes' and l₂ <+ nodes'. By IH, `(l₁.map f).sum + (l₂.map f).sum ≤ (nodes'.map f).sum ≤ f a + (nodes'.map f).sum = ((a::nodes').map f).sum`.\n\nThis proof works! The key is to extract the information that if a ∈ l and l <+ a :: nodes', then l = a :: l' where l' <+ nodes'.\n\nThis follows from the definition of Sublist:\n- If l = [] and [] <+ a :: nodes', then... well, [] is always a sublist.\n- If l = b :: l' and b :: l' <+ a :: nodes', then either b = a and l' <+ nodes', or b ≠ a and b :: l' <+ nodes'.\n\nWait, the Sublist definition:\n```\ninductive Sublist {α} : List α → List α → Prop\n  | slnil : Sublist [] []\n  | cons a : Sublist l₁ l₂ → Sublist l₁ (a :: l₂)\n  | cons_cons a : Sublist l₁ l₂ → Sublist (a :: l₁) (a :: l₂)\n```\n\nSo if `b :: l' <+ a :: nodes'`, we have two cases:\n- `cons_cons a h` where `h : l' <+ nodes'` and the lists are `a :: l'` and `a :: nodes'`. This requires `b = a`.\n- `cons a' h` where `h : b :: l' <+ nodes'`. This doesn't require `b = a`.\n\nWait, let me re-read:\n- `cons a : Sublist l₁ l₂ → Sublist l₁ (a :: l₂)` — this says if l₁ <+ l₂, then l₁ <+ a :: l₂.\n- `cons_cons a : Sublist l₁ l₂ → Sublist (a :: l₁) (a :: l₂)` — this says if l₁ <+ l₂, then a :: l₁ <+ a :: l₂.\n\nSo if `b :: l' <+ a :: nodes'`:\n- Either it's `cons a' h` where `h : b :: l' <+ nodes'` (and a' is some element, but the list being consed onto is `a :: nodes'`, so `a'` must be `a`? No, `cons a' h : Sublist l₁ (a' :: l₂)`. Here the target is `a :: nodes'`, so `a' = a`. So `h : b :: l' <+ nodes'`.)\n\nWait, I'm confused. Let me look at the Sublist definition again.\n\n`cons a : Sublist l₁ l₂ → Sublist l₁ (a :: l₂)`\n\nThis constructor takes a sublist `l₁ <+ l₂` and produces `l₁ <+ a :: l₂`. So if we have `b :: l' <+ a :: nodes'`, we could get it via `cons a h` where `h : b :: l' <+ nodes'`. Here the `a` in `cons a` is the element being consed, which must match the head of the target list. So `a` must equal `a` (the head of `a :: nodes'`). Yes, they're the same `a`.\n\nSo `cons a h : b :: l' <+ a :: nodes'` means `h : b :: l' <+ nodes'`. This is the case where the head of the target list is not part of the sublist.\n\nThe other case is `cons_cons a h : a :: l' <+ a :: nodes'` where `h : l' <+ nodes'`. This is the case where the head of the target list IS part of the sublist, and it must match the head of the source list.\n\nSo if `b :: l' <+ a :: nodes'`:\n- Either `b = a` and `l' <+ nodes'` (via `cons_cons a h`)\n- Or `b :: l' <+ nodes'` (via `cons a h`, where the `a` in `cons a` is the same as the head of the target, which is `a`)\n\nIn both cases, we get information about `b :: l'` relative to `nodes'`.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me just write the proof using the `cases` tactic on the Sublist derivation.\n\nActually, I think I should use a different approach. Instead of proving the general lemma `sum_two_disjoint_sublists_le`, I can prove a specialized version for my use case.\n\nIn my use case, I have `q <+ nodes` and `q' <+ nodes`, and they're disjoint. I need `wsum w q + wsum w q' ≤ wsum w nodes`.\n\nBut wait, I realize I can use `wsum_sublist_le` twice:\n- `wsum w q ≤ wsum w nodes` (since q <+ nodes)\n- `wsum w q' ≤ wsum w nodes` (since q' <+ nodes)\n\nBut this gives `wsum w q + wsum w q' ≤ 2 * wsum w nodes`, which is not what I need.\n\nHowever, I also know that q and q' are disjoint. This means that the elements of q and q' are distinct. So the multiset of q ++ q' has each element at most once. Since nodes is Nodup, the multiset of q ++ q' is a sub-multiset of nodes. Therefore, the sum over q ++ q' is at most the sum over nodes.\n\nBut `wsum w (q ++ q') = wsum w q + wsum w q'` (by `wsum_append`). So I need `wsum w (q ++ q') ≤ wsum w nodes`.\n\nNow, `q ++ q'` is NOT necessarily a sublist of nodes. So I can't use `wsum_sublist_le` directly.\n\nBut I can prove `wsum w (q ++ q') ≤ wsum w nodes` using the fact that q and q' are disjoint sublists of a Nodup list.\n\nLet me try to prove this by induction on the derivation of `q <+ nodes` and `q' <+ nodes`.\n\nActually, I think the easiest way is to use the following lemma, which should be in core:\n\n`List.Sublist.sum_le_sum` — but I checked and it's not there.\n\nOK let me just write the proof directly using the approach I outlined. I'll use `induction` on `nodes` and case analysis on membership.", "summary": []}
